Most important takeaways
	1. Message Queue: Decouples notification creation from delivery.
	2. Separate channel processors: Email, SMS and Push scale independently.
	3. At-least-once delivery: Ensures messages are retried, but duplicates are possible.
	4. Idempotency: Prevents duplicate notifications during retries.
	5. Exponential backoff + DLQ: Handles temporary and persistent failures.
	6. Scheduler: Supports delayed and recurring notifications.
	7. Redis: Handles preference caching and rate limiting.
	8. Queue monitoring: Queue lag is an important indicator of processing bottlenecks.
	
Interview tip: Focus on the notification delivery flow, queue architecture, retries, idempotency, rate limiting and scheduled delivery. These are the most important design challenges.

One important distinction to remember: A notification being accepted by your queue does not mean it was delivered to the user. Track separate states such as QUEUED, PROCESSING, SENT, DELIVERED and FAILED. Provider acceptance and actual device delivery are not always the same thing.
